            "old_string": "### 35.6 English anchor formalization as interlingual semantic layer\n- [x] Phase 1 — Context+Anchor signal fusion (~90 LOC):\n  - Current `TranslateOutcome { Confirmed, Rejected, Unknown }` is binary\n    and evaluates anchors independently of `context_clues`.  The real\n    precision win is fusing both signals: a translation hit WITH a local\n    context clue is far stronger than either alone.\n  - Replace `TranslateOutcome` with:\n    ```rust\n    pub struct AnchorOutcome {\n        pub confidence: f32,            // 0.0–1.0\n        pub method: ResolutionMethod,   // how confidence was derived\n        pub evidence: Option<String>,   // human/LLM-readable rationale\n    }\n    pub enum ResolutionMethod {\n        AnchorAndContext,  // translation hit AND context clue present  (1.0)\n        ExactAnchor,       // translation substring match, no clues     (0.8)\n        SynonymAnchor,     // synonym/stem hit in translation       (0.6–0.7)\n        ContextOnly,       // no translation; deterministic clues only  (0.5)\n        Rejected,          // translation succeeded, no match, no clue  (0.1)\n        NoSignal,          // translation failed; fail-open             (0.0)\n    }\n    ```\n  - Key insight: `AnchorAndContext` is the highest-confidence tier because\n    two independent signals agree.  A rule with `context_clues: [\"GPU\"]`\n    that also gets a translation containing \"rendering\" is near-certain.\n    `ExactAnchor` without clues is strong but not decisive (the translation\n    could match by coincidence).  `ContextOnly` (0.5) is intentionally in\n    the ambiguous zone — context clues alone have ~5% FP rate; anchors\n    exist precisely to reduce that.\n  - `check_anchor()` (translate.rs:447) gains a\n    `context_clue: Option<&str>` parameter (first matched clue text,\n    or `None`).  Derived at call site from `issue.context_clues` —\n    due to trigger-gate semantics, `is_some()` on an emitted issue\n    means clues matc